1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
 Testing, testing.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
 Testing, testing.

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,000
 His mic may be...

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,000
 Testing, testing.

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
 Testing, ok.

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,000
 Alright, so, how many of you have seen the show Ted Lasso before?

7
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:26,000
 For those who haven't, it is a show about an American football coach, this football coach,

8
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:31,000
 who gets hired to manage an English soccer team, the one that you use to foot.

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
 But this is a sport that he actually knows absolutely nothing about.

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
 But this show is actually more than just soccer.

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
 I don't know how many people have seen the American TV series Ted Lasso.

12
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
 It is a TV series about an American football coach.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
 He was hired to manage a British football team.

14
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
 He actually knows absolutely nothing about this sport, but this show is not just about soccer.

15
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 So I've been catching up on the latest season, season 4 of Ted Lasso recently.

16
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,000
 And one of the things that makes me come back to this show again and again

17
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,000
 is the way the show develops the story and personality of each of these individual characters.

18
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:24,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

19
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,000
 So one of the characters that has a very interesting story arc is Nathan Shelley.

20
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
 So he is the guy with the blue and red pullover there.

21
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
 When the show begins, we are being introduced to Nate as the Kidman of AFC Richmond,

22
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 which is just a polite way of saying that he does the laundry for all the players.

23
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
 So he washes the uniforms, he lays out the gears, and he picks up towels off the floor from a grown man

24
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
 who probably earns more in a week than him in an entire year.

25
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

26
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:21,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

27
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
 And in the locker room, none of the players respect him as well.

28
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,000
 And some of them just outright bully him.

29
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
 And to the owner and the management, he is pretty much invisible.

30
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
 And it seems like no one actually knows his real name.

31
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:46,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

32
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,000
 And then here comes this American coach.

33
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 And one of the very first things that Ted Lasso does when he walks into the building

34
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,000
 is to ask Nate about his name, not just "Hey, Kidman," as all the other players do.

35
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
 And not only that, Ted actually remembers his name.

36
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,000
 And he calls him the next day and the next day.

37
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:21,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

38
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:27,000
 And one day, Ted asks him a question that nobody in the entire organization has even bothered to ask him.

39
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:32,000
 He asks Nate about his opinion on the soccer team and how they play.

40
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:42,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

41
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
 And it turns out that the guy who washes the smelly and sweaty laundry

42
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
 has been standing at the edge of every training session for years, watching.

43
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
 And so he understands the game better than almost anyone in the building.

44
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
 And so Ted started to listen to him, utilizing his ideas in matches with positive results.

45
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
 And by the end of that first season, Nate isn't the Kidman anymore.

46
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
 He is being promoted to the assistant coach.

47
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:32,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

48
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
 Yeah, so this is a picture of him being promoted as a coach.

49
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

50
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
 And here's something that really stood out to me.

51
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:49,000
 Nate always has the technical knowledge about soccer in him, even before Ted Lasso arrived.

52
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:55,000
 And all he needs is just that one opportunity for someone to finally see him and value him as a person,

53
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,000
 instead of just what he does for the club that is the Kidman.

54
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:21,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

55
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
 And so we have been walking through the book of Judges for a while now,

56
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 and watching how Israel slowly descends into spiritual and moral downfall.

57
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
 Today we come to the second to last major judge in the book.

58
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:40,000
 And the specific judge that we are going to look at today actually reminds me a lot about Nate from Ted Lasso,

59
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
 being overlooked and rejected, and yet somehow he found his way to the top.

60
00:05:45,000 --> 00:06:05,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

61
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
 But before we get into our passage, let me remind us where we have been,

62
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:15,000
 because today's passage sits at a specific point in the spiritual downfall of Israel.

63
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:27,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

64
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
 So the entire book of Judges, again, it opens with Israel settling for half-hearted obedience.

65
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:39,000
 They don't fully drive out the Canaanites, and before we know, they already start worshipping the Canaanite gods.

66
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:50,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

67
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:56,000
 And then the cycle starts turning. Israel sins, God hands them over to an oppressor, they cry out,

68
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:01,000
 God raises up for them a judge, and then the land has rest. And then they will do it all over again.

69
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:13,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

70
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
 And the first few judges that we look at, they are very solid.

71
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,000
 And when the story ends, things actually go back to normal in Israel.

72
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:29,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

73
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:35,000
 And then came Gideon, and in my last two sermons, we watched the rock being pulled underneath of Israel.

74
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:40,000
 God used Gideon powerfully, yet he ended his life making a golden ephah,

75
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
 which led the whole nation to idol worship.

76
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:49,000
 And so Gideon is the pivot point, the first judge whose own lifetime actually dragged Israel backwards.

77
00:07:49,000 --> 00:08:15,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

78
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:19,000
 And so today we're going to meet the judge who comes next after Gideon.

79
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:26,000
 His name is Jephthah. And I warned you before, his story is actually one of the hardest in this book.

80
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
 And there are so many things that we can unpack.

81
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:40,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

82
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:47,000
 So we're going to take two Sundays with him. Today is actually part one, so today is the good news part.

83
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
 Next time is part two, and that will be the warning.

84
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:58,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

85
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
 So, let me set the scenes.

86
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:07,000
 So, Judges chapter 10 tells us that Israel has fallen into a whole new level of idolatry.

87
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:13,000
 And this time, the text here in verse 6, it actually lists out seven different sets of gods,

88
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:18,000
 not individual gods, but seven different sets of gods that Israel worship.

89
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:30,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

90
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:35,000
 So God, of course, hands them over to the oppressor, the Ammonites,

91
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
 and for 18 years they are being oppressed.

92
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:44,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

93
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:50,000
 And then Israel cries out, and here's what makes this cycle actually different from the one before.

94
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,000
 God actually says, "No."

95
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:58,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

96
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:03,000
 So, look at verse 13 and 14. This is God's response to Israel.

97
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:15,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

98
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:28,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

99
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:33,000
 Church, this is the most severe thing that God has said to Israel up to this point in this entire book.

100
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:41,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

101
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:46,000
 And by the time we reach the end of chapter 10, Israel is desperate.

102
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
 We learn that the Ammonites, they are camped and ready to attack.

103
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:56,000
 And in the last verse, in verse 18, the leaders of Gilead, they're standing around and asking each other these questions.

104
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:03,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

105
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:23,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

106
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:27,000
 But, nobody volunteers.

107
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:31,000
 And this is the moment where our passage begins here in Genesis 11.

108
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:39,000
 Israel, they have no leader, no options, and also a God who just told them that He is done with rescuing them.

109
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:44,000
 And it is in this setting that we are being introduced to Japheth.

110
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:56,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

111
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:04,000
 So, Japheth is a mighty warrior, but he was born into shame.

112
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:08,000
 Verse 1 tells that Japheth was the son of a prostitute.

113
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:14,000
 And in that culture, that label actually follows you for your entire life.

114
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:23,000
 And it actually did here for Japheth, because verse 2 tells us that his half-brothers actually kicked him out of his family with this word.

115
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:30,000
 So in verse 2, you are not going to get any inheritance in our family, because you are the son of another woman.

116
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:35,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

117
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:55,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

118
00:12:55,000 --> 00:13:02,000
 And notice who rejected him here. It is not strangers or the Ammonites, but it is his own family.

119
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:16,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

120
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:22,000
 And one scholar pointed out this moment, probably had taught Japheth the power of words.

121
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:29,000
 So I want you to hold on to that, because the man who was destroyed by somebody else's words,

122
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:35,000
 in our part 2, next time, he is going to destroy his own family with the words that he speaks.

123
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:50,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

124
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:57,000
 So, in verse 3, he ran to a place called Tov, which is actually the word for "good" in Hebrew.

125
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:06,000
 And yet he actually found nothing good here, because for Japheth, this so-called land of good actually became the place of exile and rejection.

126
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:22,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

127
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:30,000
 So look at who he ended up in, in verse 3 right here. He ended up with a gang of scoundrels gathered around him and followed him.

128
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:34,000
 And again, Hebrew literally calls these people "empty men".

129
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:40,000
 And just a few quick words, the word doesn't necessarily mean that they were immoral.

130
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:47,000
 It just means that these were men with nothing. No property, no status, and no place.

131
00:14:47,000 --> 00:15:10,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

132
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:21,000
 But here's Japheth when we meet him here. He is illegitimate, disowned by his family, homeless, and running a gang of nobodies out in the wild.

133
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:34,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

134
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:44,000
 But the story is about to take a 180. If you remember at the end of chapter 10, the Ammonites, they were camping outside of the city ready to attack.

135
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:50,000
 And remember how God told Israel to cry out to the false gods for help instead.

136
00:15:50,000 --> 00:16:03,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

137
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:13,000
 And so, out of desperation, the very elders of Gilead, who actually allowed his family to disown him, now actually come crawling back asking for help.

138
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:21,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

139
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:30,000
 So, verse 4. "Some time later, when the Ammonites were fighting against Israel, the elders of Gilead went to Japheth from the land of Tov.

140
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:35,000
 'Come,' they said, 'be our commander so we can fight the Ammonites.'

141
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:51,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

142
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:57,000
 So, pay attention to their opening offer here. 'Be our commander in the battlefield.'

143
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:05,000
 This is actually just a temporary job and not a permanent position. But Japheth does not let them off the hook.

144
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:17,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

145
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:23,000
 So, verse 7. "Japheth said to them, 'Didn't you hate me and drive me from my father's house?

146
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:26,000
 Why do you come to me now when you are in trouble?'"

147
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:34,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

148
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:40,000
 So Japheth, he is not being petty here. He is just calling out what is actually happening.

149
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:44,000
 These elders, they didn't come back to him because they realized that they were wrong.

150
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:50,000
 But they actually came back to him out of desperation because the enemy was at the door.

151
00:17:50,000 --> 00:18:04,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

152
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:09,000
 So, the elders come back with an improved offer. So, in verse 8.

153
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:20,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

154
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:32,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

155
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:38,000
 So, this time, their offer is actually a permanent position that comes with real authority.

156
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:46,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

157
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:59,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

158
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
 So, look at his response in verse 9.

159
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:14,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

160
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:31,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

161
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:40,000
 So, notice here that for Japheth, besides delivering Israel, he has a self-serving motive of being reinstated as the leader.

162
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:44,000
 Only then does he agree on his own term to fight the Ammonites.

163
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:50,000
 And this selfish and self-serving motive will continue to play out in the rest of his life.

164
00:19:50,000 --> 00:20:10,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

165
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
 So, let's pause here for a moment.

166
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:20,000
 If you have been following along my judges series, you might recognize one big glaring red flag right here.

167
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:29,000
 Because up to this point in judges, every judge that comes before him, they were raised up by God himself.

168
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:36,000
 However, Japheth is actually headhunted by a group of desperate elders who couldn't find anyone else better.

169
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:41,000
 And his appointment is explicitly conditional on him winning the battle.

170
00:20:41,000 --> 00:21:07,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

171
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:12,000
 So, as the story continues, this question that hangs over everything is this.

172
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:19,000
 Does God actually want this man? Or did Israel just pick somebody and hope for the best?

173
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:25,000
 After all, this champion that Israel chose, Japheth, he is a reject.

174
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:32,000
 And he only accepts his task with his own personal agenda in mind, that he wants to be reinstated as the leader.

175
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:55,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

176
00:21:55,000 --> 00:22:02,000
 So, despite all the things that we know about Japheth so far, here comes verse 29.

177
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:13,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

178
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:28,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

179
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:35,000
 So, Church, do you feel the shock of that? God doesn't just say, "Actually, I never chose this man."

180
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:40,000
 Or he doesn't wait for Japheth to clean up his background, or to make peace with his brothers,

181
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:44,000
 or dismiss his gang of empty members.

182
00:22:44,000 --> 00:23:00,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

183
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:09,000
 And God actually takes this man they judge, the illegitimate son, the outcast, and He fills him with His own spirit.

184
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:18,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

185
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:25,000
 While his family said, "You get nothing here," and his community said, "We don't have other choice. You will do for now."

186
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:31,000
 Yet God said to him, "Let me use this guy and fill him with My spirit."

187
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:38,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

188
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:42,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

189
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:45,000
 So, this is the main idea that I want us to take away with us today.

190
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:49,000
 That God uses broken people to accomplish His purpose.

191
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:58,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

192
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:04,000
 Maybe some of you have been carrying something for years that you were convinced that it disqualifies you.

193
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:09,000
 Something in your family that you didn't choose, something in your past that you did,

194
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:17,000
 or a season you were not proud of, or a label that somebody puts on you when you were young and you were not able to shake it off.

195
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:42,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

196
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:50,000
 And I want you to share this this morning, that the Lord pours out His Spirit on someone where everybody else has written off of.

197
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:57,000
 So, no matter what kind of family you come from, or what kind of wound you carry, or mistakes that you have from your past,

198
00:24:57,000 --> 00:25:02,000
 none of it gets in the final words on what God can do through you.

199
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:06,000
 Because our past is not bigger than God's willingness to use us.

200
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:09,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

201
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:13,000
 So, back to the story with Nate from Ted Lasso.

202
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:20,000
 If you have seen the first few seasons, you probably know that Nate's story actually tells us a lot about the past.

203
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:30,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

204
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:33,000
 So, back to the story with Nate from Ted Lasso.

205
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:41,000
 If you have seen the first few seasons, you probably know that Nate's story actually takes a turn that is kind of hard to watch.

206
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:50,000
 The man who is finally being seen and given real authority, he becomes a man who actually hurts people.

207
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:56,000
 And what makes it so painful is that the wound from his past, it actually never healed.

208
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:59,000
 It just got power behind it right now.

209
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:25,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

210
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:31,000
 So, keep that in mind, because next time we will pick up from after the Spirit comes on Jephthah.

211
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:37,000
 And we're going to watch the very same thing that happened to Nate actually happen to Jephthah as well.

212
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:40,000
 But that is part two, and that is for next time.

213
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:46,000
 And this morning, I want us to focus on this, that God uses broken people to accomplish His purpose.

214
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

215
00:26:48,000 --> 00:27:03,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

216
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:11,000
 If you turn to the first chapter in the New Testament, Matthew chapter 1, you will actually find the genealogy of Jesus.

217
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:17,000
 And I know for some people, when they flip through a genealogy in the Bible, they usually just skip right through it.

218
00:27:17,000 --> 00:27:25,000
 But right in the family line of Jesus, the Messiah, we actually find this one name, Rahab.

219
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:29,000
 And if you don't know, she is also a prostitute.

220
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:41,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

221
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:51,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

222
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:57,000
 And so the very reason that Jephthah's family used to write him out of the story,

223
00:27:57,000 --> 00:28:03,000
 God actually deliberately wrote into the lineage of His own son, Jesus.

224
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:15,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

225
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:24,000
 And not only that, for Jesus Himself, He knew what it was like to be rejected and betrayed by His own people.

226
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:26,000
 So He sees us.

227
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:29,000
 And yet Jesus endures it all the way to the cross.

228
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:36,000
 And as the stone that the Buddha has rejected, God says that He has become the cornerstone.

229
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:52,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

230
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:56,000
 So in a moment, we are going to take communion together in response to our message,

231
00:28:56,000 --> 00:29:00,000
 and to remember Jesus, the rejected cornerstone.

232
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:03,000
 And as we prepare our heart, I want you to hear this.

233
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:16,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

234
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:21,000
 If you have been carrying something for years that you are sure put you out of reach,

235
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:24,000
 the God of the Bible, the God that we worship,

236
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:31,000
 has a long and consistent history of building His kingdom out of materials that everybody else discarded.

237
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:35,000
 And our past is not bigger than His grace.

238
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:50,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

239
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:55,000
 And the greatest evidence of grace is Jesus Himself,

240
00:29:55,000 --> 00:30:00,000
 whose body was broken for us, and whose blood was shed for us.

241
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:05,000
 And so when we take the bread and the cup in communion, remember Jesus.

242
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:17,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

243
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:24,000
 And just a reminder, the bread and the cup are reserved for those who truly follow Jesus.

244
00:30:24,000 --> 00:30:28,000
 If you don't identify yourself as a follower of Jesus yet,

245
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:31,000
 I would encourage you to actually take this time to reflect,

246
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:35,000
 and consider what it means to surrender your life to follow Him.

247
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:52,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

248
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:56,000
 And if you are ready, you can come up. We have the table here with the elements.

249
00:30:56,000 --> 00:31:01,000
 You can grab it, go back to your seat, and after that we will take the bread and the cup together.

250
00:31:01,000 --> 00:31:04,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

251
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:07,000
 [Music]

252
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:11,000
 [Music]

253
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:38,000
 [Music]

254
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:42,000
 Let's take the bread together. This is the body of Jesus broken for us.

255
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:54,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

256
00:31:54,000 --> 00:31:59,000
 And the cup is the blood of Jesus shed for us. So let's take it together.

257
00:31:59,000 --> 00:32:06,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

258
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:09,000
 Let's pray together.

259
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:15,000
 Father, thank you for being a God who is gracious and compassionate.

260
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:19,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

261
00:32:19,000 --> 00:32:27,000
 You don't disqualify us because of our past, but you send your son Jesus to redeem us from it.

262
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:33,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

263
00:32:33,000 --> 00:32:38,000
 Jesus, thank you for being a God who welcomes sinners and others.

264
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:44,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

265
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:48,000
 And through your blood and your body, we can be made new again.

266
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:52,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

267
00:32:52,000 --> 00:32:57,000
 And Holy Spirit, thank you for being the power of God who moves in us.

268
00:32:57,000 --> 00:33:02,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

269
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:07,000
 By your power, make us a people who see others the way you do.

270
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:13,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

271
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:16,000
 And in Jesus' name we pray. Amen.

272
00:33:16,000 --> 00:33:18,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

273
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:21,000
 Alright, thank you all for joining us this morning.

274
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:27,000
 I would encourage you to stay after watching this and have some fellowship with the Lord.

275
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:36,000
 [Translated from Chinese]

276
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:39,000
 Alright, God bless you all. God bless.

